==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*wurdiz}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*wrti-}}, a verbal abstract from the root {{m|ine-pro|*wert-||to turn}} (whence Latin {{m|la|vertere}}), related to the Old English verb {{m|ang|weorþan||to grow into, become}} (compare Dutch {{m|nl|worden}}, German {{m|de|werden}}). Cognate with Old Saxon {{m|osx|wurd}}, Old High German {{m|goh|wurt}}, Old Norse {{m|non|urðr}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/wyrd/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|f}}

# [[fate]], [[destiny]]
#: ''Gæð a wyrd swa hio scel.'' Fate always goes as she must. (''Beowulf'' l. 455)
# {{lb|ang|in the plural}} the [[Fates]]
# [[event]], [[occurrence]]

